WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.340
You grip a bar and look at it.

2
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.220
Your eyes and your hand disagree about its height.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.200
Which do you believe?

4
00:00:10.983 --> 00:00:12.563
These numbers are made up.

5
00:00:13.303 --> 00:00:14.763
Your eyes say 50 mm.

6
00:00:15.683 --> 00:00:16.683
Your hand says 54

7
00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:19.953
Each sense is a ruler that wobbles.

8
00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:21.973
Here vision wobbles less.

9
00:00:22.853 --> 00:00:24.993
Its standard deviation is 2 mm.

10
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:29.217
Touch wobbles more, with a standard deviation of 4

11
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.020
Predict. Should one combined answer land halfway, or nearer one sense?

12
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.593
It lands much nearer vision, at 50.8 mm.

13
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:49.093
Each weight is set by reliability, one over the variance.

14
00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:51.793
Vision gets a weight of 0.8.

15
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:54.213
Read it another way.

16
00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:57.453
Start at vision, then move a fifth of the way toward touch.

17
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:05.483
The combined answer is also steadier than either sense alone, if their errors are

18
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:06.483
independent.

19
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:11.023
Now blur the picture, until vision wobbles twice as much as touch.

20
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:14.383
Where does the answer go?

21
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:28.953
The weights swap. The answer moves to 53.2 mm, much nearer touch.

22
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:33.037
Alais and Burr found this reversal for sound and sight.

23
00:01:33.957 --> 00:01:36.557
Severe blur let sound capture the perceived location.

24
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:42.507
Ernst and Banks tested the rule in four people, judging bar height by sight and by

25
00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:43.507
touch.

26
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:48.557
Predictions built from each person's single-sense judgements closely resembled the

27
00:01:48.557 --> 00:01:49.557
combined ones.

28
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.930
One task, four people, a behavioural fit.

29
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.130
It does not show where in the brain.

30
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.330
Now one more fact.

31
00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.490
Some of what your senses report is caused by you.

32
00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:02.813
Here is the one idea.

33
00:02:02.813 --> 00:02:06.313
A signal is worth what it tells you about the world.

34
00:02:07.217 --> 00:02:10.257
For one event, the more reliable sense counts for more.

35
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.980
Part of what you sense is the predictable result of your own movement.

36
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.040
Some pathways subtract a prediction of it.

37
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.310
Take this with you.

38
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:22.570
Weigh each sense by its reliability.

39
00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.290
Then ask which predictable results of your own movement get subtracted.
